   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the state finance law and the real property tax law, in\nrelation to authorizing certain municipalities in a pilot emergency repair\nprogram to add unpaid emergency repair charges and housing code violation\npenalties, costs and fines to such municipalities' annual tax levy in\naccordance with applicable law; and providing for the repeal of certain\nprovisions of the state finance law upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8500",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8500-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 41,
       "fullName": "Michelle Hinchey",
       "imgName": "1314_Michelle_Hinchey.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1497,
       "sessionMemberId": 2047,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "HINCHEY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §54-g, St Fin L; add §903, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8500\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state finance law and the real property tax law, in\nrelation to authorizing certain municipalities in a pilot emergency\nrepair program to add unpaid emergency repair charges and housing code\nviolation penalties, costs  and fines to such municipalities' annual tax\nlevy in accordance with applicable law; and providing for the repeal of\ncertain provisions of the state finance law upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo create a pilot emergency repair program to allow cities to directly\naddress code violations where the owner has not undertaken repairs.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Directs that the secretary of state, with assistance from the\ndivision of housing and community renewal, to establish a pilot program\nfor activities related to the administration and enforcement of the fire\nprevention and building codes. The program will permit participating\nmunicipalities to immediately repair hazardous code violations in build-\nings where the owner has not undertaken repairs, and permit them to bill\nthe owner for repair costs. In the event payment is not received within\nthirty days, the secretary shall reimburse the municipality for such\nrepairs. The State then will be forwarded monies due from the owner for\nsuch repairs once they are collected. Only municipalities designated as\na city will be eligible to participate in the pilot program.\n\nSection 2: Outlines the guidelines of the pilot program.\n\n1) Authorizes participating municipalities to collect unpaid emergency\nrepair charges, housing, building, and fire code violation penalties.\n\n2) Outlines that eligible charges, penalties, costs, and fines must be\nimposed through a judgment in court and must have remained unpaid for\none year after the final adjudication and exhaustion of all appeals.\n\n3) Sets minimum amount to be eligible for program: at least five percent\nof the amount of the tax assessed value of the property.\n\n4) Directs that the unpaid charges be set down in the annual tax levy as\nuncollected fines and penalties.\n\n5) Directs that the participating municipality must notify all owners or\nknown interested parties of unpaid emergency repair charges and/or code\nviolations on the municipal tax levy as uncollected fines and penalties\nwithin thirty days of placement.\n\n6) States that unpaid emergency repair charges shall be placed on the\ntax year that the municipality is currently in.\n\n7) Exempts a residential dwelling that is owner-occupied or is the\nprimary residence of a homeowner from the program.\n\n8) Directs that the participating municipality shall develop a program\nto assist tenants residing in a dwelling at risk of tax foreclosure\nbefore placing a property on the tax levy. Program will include housing\ncounseling assistance or other relocation support to suitable housing.\n\n9) Permits creation of payment plans for owners with participating muni-\ncipality for past amounts due for emergency repair charges and/or code\nviolations.\n\n10) Outlines curing code violations: if all violations that led to\npenalties, fees, and costs are corrected prior to the expiration period\nfor redemption, then the property shall be removed from the levy and\nauction and balance of amount owed will be placed as a lien on the prop-\nerty.\n\n11) States that a property may not be auctioned and will be removed from\nthe tax levy should the outstanding balance be paid on a property's\nemergency repair costs and if there are also no unpaid real property\ntaxes\n\n12) States that any surplus funds from a sale of a property at a tax\nforeclosure for unpaid code violations shall be returned to the former\nowner of the property. This provision does not apply to sales of proper-\nty at a tax foreclosure due to unpaid taxes.\n\n13) Should there be emergency repair charges and/or code violations\nstill due after the auction of the property, the municipality may\nproceed with action against the former owner pursuant to applicable laws\n\n14) Excludes municipalities that sell their tax liens in a tax lien sale\nto the provisions of the program.\n\nSection 3: Sets effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAround New York State there are a properties in cities that have been\nissued code violations by the jurisdictional municipality but continue\nto be in a state of disrepair either due to negligence or unwillingness\nby the owner to make the necessary repairs. This can create extreme\nsafety issues to renters who reside at the property and slowly deteri-\norates property values in the surrounding neighborhood.\n\nThis bill seeks to remedy this issue by creating a program that would\nauthorize participating municipalities to make direct code enforcement\nrepairs that the landlord has either failed or refused to do. The muni-\ncipality places the cost of the emergency repairs on the landlord's\nproperty tax bill as a lien, with the state providing the municipality\nwith the upfront costs of the repairs and recouping the money upon reme-\ndy of the lien/violations. A similar program currently exists in NYC\nunder its Housing Preservation and Development's Alternative Enforcement\nProgram through issuing of Orders to Correct.\n\nThe pilot program created by this bill will last until May 1, 2026.\nDuring which the secretary of state in conjunction with the division of\nhousing and community renewal will file a report annually to evaluate\nthe effectiveness of the pilot program with the legislature and the\ngovernor.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nSenate\n\n2023: S8146A, Vetoed\n\n2021-2022: S8146A, Passed Senate\n\nAssembly\n\n2023: A1662-A, Vetoed\n\n2021-2022: A9504A, Referred to Local Governments\n\n2021-2022: A9504A, Amend and recommit to Local Governments\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided, however, section one\nof this act shall expire and be deemed repealed May 1, 2026\n",
