    "lawCode": "Add §99-aa, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8500A\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to authorizing\ncertain municipalities to establish an emergency repair pilot program;\nand providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo create a pilot emergency repair program to allow cities to directly\naddress code violations where the owner has not undertaken repairs.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Directs that the Secretary of State, with assistance from the\nDivision of Housing and Community Renewal to establish a pilot program\nin which participating cities will be required to adopt a local law that\nwill permit them to immediately repair hazardous code violations in\nbuildings where the owner has not undertaken repairs and permit them to\nbill the owner for repair costs. The Secretary of State shall, subject\nto appropriations, provide technical assistance and support to partic-\nipating cities to ensure successful implementation of these local law as\nwell as publish a model local law that participating cities may adopt.\nThe pilot program created by this bill will last until May 1, 2027.\nDuring which the secretary of state in conjunction with the division of\nhousing and community renewal will file a report annually to evaluate\nthe effectiveness of the pilot program with the legislature and the\ngovernor.\n\nSection 2: Sets effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAround New York State there are a properties in cities that have been\nissued code violations by the jurisdictional municipality but continue\nto be in a state of disrepair either due to negligence or unwillingness\nby the owner to make the necessary repairs. This can create extreme\nsafety issues to renters who reside at the property and slowly deteri-\norates property values in the surrounding neighborhood. This bill seeks\nto remedy this issue by creating a program that would authorize partic-\nipating municipalities to make direct code enforcement repairs that the\nlandlord has either failed or refused to do.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nSenate\n\n2023: S5207B, Vetoed\n\n2021-2022: S 8146-A; Passed Senate\n\nAssembly\n\n2023: A1662A, Vetoed\n\n2021-22: A9504; Amend and recommit to local governments\n\n \nFISCAL LIMITATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law and shall expire and be deemed repealed May 1, 2027.\n",
